<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · religion</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bogomilism</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">10th–15th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> · Also known as Bogomils</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bogomilism was a dualist religious movement founded in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a> in the 10th century, during the reign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tsar">Tsar</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-i-duke-of-brittany">Peter I</a>, by a priest named <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bogomil">Bogomil</a>. Its followers rejected the church hierarchy, the sacraments and the material world, which they held to be the work of an evil principle. Condemned by church and state, it spread to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantium</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosnia">Bosnia</a> and influenced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catharism">Cathars</a> of western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>.</p></div>
